<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kriegsmarine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1935">1935</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kriegsmarine was the navy of Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> from 1935 to 1945, successor to the Reichsmarine. Much smaller than the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>, it relied in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> on surface raiders and above all on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/u-boat">U-boats</a>, which sank thousands of Allied merchant ships in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-atlantic">Battle of the Atlantic</a> before being defeated in 1943. Its commanders were Erich Raeder and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-donitz">Karl Dönitz</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kriegsmarine.jpg" alt="Kriegsmarine" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lancaster, F l a n k e r · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kriegsmarine_insignia_casco.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Under the 1919 Treaty of Versailles Germany was allowed a minimal navy of 15,000 personnel, six capital ships of no more than 10,000 tons, six cruisers, twelve destroyers and twelve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torpedo">torpedo</a> boats, with no submarines, aircraft carriers or naval aviation. The ships and men were taken over from the Kaiserliche Marine, which was renamed the Reichsmarine. Germany worked from the outset to circumvent the limits, keeping up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/u-boat">U-boat</a> design through an office in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and developing the G7e torpedo in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. On 15 November 1932 the government decided on a prohibited naval re-armament.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-german-naval-agreement">Anglo-German Naval Agreement</a> of 18 June 1935 allowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> a navy equal to 35 per cent of British surface tonnage and 45 per cent of British submarine tonnage, and that year the Reichsmarine became the Kriegsmarine. Its first military action came in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>, when ships were sent to Spanish waters from July 1936 under the cover of non-intervention while covertly supporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-franco">Francisco Franco</a>'s Nationalists. After Republican bombers attacked the Deutschland off Ibiza on 29 May 1937, killing 31, the Admiral Scheer shelled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almeria</a>, killing 19 or 20 civilians.</p><p>In January 1939 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> approved Plan Z, a programme of about 800 ships for 1939 to 1947, built around six H-class battleships and meant to give parity with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>. The outbreak of war in September 1939 shelved it in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/u-boat">U-boats</a>, and resources went first to the army and air force. In the first months the Kriegsmarine sank the carrier HMS Courageous and the battleship HMS Royal Oak, and lost the Admiral Graf Spee at the Battle of the River Plate.</p><p>Wolfpacks of submarines attacked British convoys in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-atlantic">Battle of the Atlantic</a>, but the tactic was largely abandoned by May 1943 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/u-boat">U-boat</a> losses mounted. After the failure at the Battle of the Barents Sea, Hitler concluded that large warships were useless, and Erich Raeder was replaced as commander-in-chief by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-donitz">Karl Dönitz</a> on 30 January 1943. Dönitz became President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> on Hitler's suicide in April 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Hitler was supreme commander, exercising authority through the Oberkommando der Marine. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a> held a near-monopoly on military aviation, a source of rivalry with the navy. Kriegsmarine personnel also manned coastal artillery, flak batteries and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radar">radar</a>. From mid-1944 surplus personnel were formed into about 40 land regiments and, from January 1945, six divisions. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> surrendered, the surviving ships were divided among the victors. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liepaja">Liepāja</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>, in July 1941, its commandants ordered hostages shot and issued anti-Jewish regulations.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kriegsmarine” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Kriegsmarine</b>’s U-boat campaign was defeated in the Atlantic, and after the Allied landings in Normandy in June 1944 the Wehrmacht was pressed back on every front.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Military</a></li></ul></section>
